OUT='/tmp/kh/extra/'
def F(name,w,cap,text): return text.strip()+'\n\n\\appfig{%s}{%s}{%s}\n\n'%(name,w,cap)
HEAD=r"\subsection{The output of the application, panel by panel}"+"\n"
# ---------------- chapter 11
s=HEAD+r"""The surface-irrigation layer of SWAFL is organised as one long page that answers the design question from the top down: the inputs, the picture of the advance, the water balance, the performance indices and a table of improvements. The figures of this section are the panels of that page for one worked case, a border strip of $L=150$ m on the coarse loam S01 with $S_0=0.20$ per cent, an inflow $q=1.05$ L\,s$^{-1}$ per metre of width and a net depth $Z_n=72$ mm taken from the daily soil-water simulation (DSS) of the application. Every number in them can be reproduced with the equations of this chapter."""+"\n\n"
s+=F('sid_inputs','0.96',r"Inputs of the surface-irrigation layer: start from the soil or from a tabulated design, choose border, furrow or basin, the intake (from the soil, or a given $k$ and $A$) and the cut-off rule, then set length, slope, inflow, roughness, farm supply and field width.",r"The top of the page holds every choice. \emph{Start from} selects the soil of the explorer or one of the stored designs (A7 border, A9 furrow, A11 border, D1 design). \emph{System} sets the geometry of the flow (Section~11.2), \emph{Intake} decides whether the Kostiakov law is built from the texture (Section~11.8) or typed, and \emph{Cutoff} chooses between the full-run rule ($E_s=1$), the 85-per-cent rule, a manual time and the exact curve. The numerical boxes below are the variables of the volume balance: field length $L$, slope $S_0$, inflow $q$, Manning roughness $n$, required depth $Z_n$ (here 72 mm, read from the DSS and not typed), the cutback after the front arrives, the farm supply $Q_s$, the field width and the organic matter that enters the pedotransfer functions. The cut-off time (401 min) is set by the rule and is shown as a result, not an input.")
s+=F('sid_plan','0.96',r"Layout in plan: the advance front (here 50 m after 27 min of the 401 min run), shaded by the depth already infiltrated.",r"The plan view draws the border from the supply canal at the left to the tail drain at the right. The colours show what the front has left behind: dry (not yet reached), \emph{short} (wetted but less than $Z_n$), \emph{filled} (between $Z_n$ and the tolerance) and \emph{excess} (more than needed, lost below the roots). The blue line marks the front, and the clock under the drawing is the time since the start. This is the picture of the quantity $x(t)$ obtained from the volume-balance Eq.~(\ref{eq:s-vb}).")
s+=F('sid_section','0.96',r"Longitudinal section: water running over the soil and soaking in, with the field divided into short, filled and excess sections and the six result cards.",r"The section shows the same run along the length. The surface stream is drawn above the grains of the soil, the wetting front is the line below, and the three labelled depths are $Z_0$ (the depth infiltrated at the inlet, 95 mm), $Z_L$ (at the tail, 72 mm) and $Z_n$ (72 mm). The bar under the picture divides the length into sections: here the whole field is \emph{excess}, so the water that goes below $Z_n$ is lost as deep percolation. The cards give the balance of the irrigation in volumes per metre of width: the required volume $V_{req}=Z_nL$ (10.77 m$^3$), the volume infiltrated (12.50 m$^3$), the volume stored in the roots (10.77 m$^3$), deep percolation (1.73 m$^3$), runoff (12.78 m$^3$) and the deficit (0). These are the quantities $V_{st}$, $V_{dp}$ and $V_{ro}$ of Eq.~(\ref{eq:s-ind}).")
s+=F('sid_hydro','0.96',r"Hydrograph of the run: inflow $q$, infiltration, runoff and surface storage through time, with the advance time $t_L=119$ min and the cut-off at 401 min.",r"The hydrograph integrates the story in time. The horizontal line is the inflow $q=1.05$ L\,s$^{-1}$ per metre. Under it, light blue is the water that fills the surface while the front advances, green is the infiltration, which rises as the wetted length grows and then decays with the intake of the soil, and brown is the runoff that leaves at the tail after $t_L$. After cut-off the inflow falls to zero, the surface drains (the brown triangle is the recession) and the infiltration finishes. The area under each curve is one of the volumes of the cards above.")
s+=F('sid_cross','0.96',r"Cross-section at the inlet: the wetted bulb in the soil, and the Green--Ampt intake curve fitted to $Z=k\,t^{A}$.",r"The left picture draws the flow area $A_0=0.0337$ m$^2$ per metre of width (sheet depth $y=3.4$ cm, drawn four times larger) and the soil wetted under it. The right graph is the intake of this soil. The dots are the Green--Ampt cumulative infiltration computed from $K_s=13.1$ mm\,h$^{-1}$, $\psi_f=16$ mm and $\Delta\theta=0.21$ (Section~11.8); the line is the Kostiakov fit $Z=0.82\,t^{0.79}$ mm with $R^2=0.997$. The dashed red line is the required depth $Z_n$ and the dotted line reads off the opportunity time $t_n=283$ min that the soil needs to take it.")
s+=F('sid_advance','0.96',r"Advance and opportunity time: the advance curve $t_{adv}(x)$ (blue), the cut-off time (red) and the intake opportunity time $\tau(x)=t_{cutoff}-t_{adv}(x)$.",r"This panel is the heart of the volume-balance method. The blue curve is the time at which the front reaches each distance; it ends at $t_L=119$ min at $x=150$ m. The red dashed line is the cut-off (401 min), so the vertical distance between them is the time the soil at that point stays under water, the opportunity time $\tau(x)$: 373 min at $x=50$ m and 282 min at the tail. The text box repeats the rule that fixes the cut-off, $t_n=(Z_n/k)^{1/A}=283$ min and $t_{co}=t_L+t_n=401$ min.")
s+=F('sid_profile','0.96',r"Infiltrated depth along the field against the required depth $Z_n$ (72 mm): the straight line from $Z_0=95$ mm to $Z_L=72$ mm.",r"The depth infiltrated is drawn as a straight line from the inlet to the tail, as constructed in the text. The green area is the water stored in the root zone ($\le Z_n$), the blue area above the dashed line is deep percolation, and any red area under the line would be the deficit. In this run the whole line is above $Z_n$, so the deficit is zero and the loss is shared between percolation and runoff.")
s+=F('sid_perf','0.96',r"Performance indices and the water balance of one irrigation: $E_a=42.6$ per cent, $E_s=100$ per cent, $DP=6.8$ per cent, $RO=50.5$ per cent, $CU=93.1$ per cent.",r"The bars are the five indicators of Eq.~(\ref{eq:s-ind}). The coloured bar at the right splits the applied volume (25.28 m$^3$ per metre, which is $q\,t_{co}$) into stored (green), deep percolation (blue) and runoff (brown). The verdict under the bars is the rule of the application: an application efficiency below 60 per cent is \emph{poor}, and the text says which loss dominates. Here the layout meets the requirement completely ($E_s=100$ per cent) but 51 per cent of the water leaves as runoff, so the page sends the user to the design table below.")
s+=F('sid_dtable','0.96',r"Design table: iterations toward an application efficiency of 85 per cent, changing one variable at each step; the green row meets the target.",r"The table is the optimiser of the layer. Row 0 is the current layout. Each following row changes one thing: row 1 cuts the inflow off earlier (297 min), row 2 lowers the inflow to 0.70 L\,s$^{-1}$, and row 3 adds a cutback to 20 per cent of the inflow after the front arrives. The columns give the inflow, length, cutback, cut-off, $E_a$, $E_s$, $DP$ and $RO$. The last row reaches $E_a=88.9$ per cent with $E_s=84$ per cent, which satisfies the rule that the root zone stays at least 80 per cent refilled; tapping the row applies it to the inputs. This is the numerical form of the improvements discussed in Section~11.11.")
s+=F('sid_sens','0.96',r"What the efficiency is most sensitive to: the change of $E_a$ when one input moves and all others are held.",r"Each bar is the change of $E_a$ (in percentage points) when one input is varied by $\pm20$ per cent (the intake exponent $A$ by $\pm10$ per cent, roughness $n$ and slope $S_0$ by $\pm30$ per cent). The intake exponent $A$ dominates ($\pm15.3$ points), then the intake coefficient $k$ ($\pm6.7$), the inflow $q$ ($\pm5.8$) and the length $L$ ($\pm5.3$); roughness, required depth and slope matter little. Green raises the efficiency and red lowers it. The ranking tells the designer which measurement is worth making carefully: the infiltration test, not the slope survey.")
s+=F('sid_meaning','0.96',r"What the design means for the season and the farm: irrigations, gross water, units run at once, sets and pumping hours.",r"The last table converts the design into farm terms. With the supply of 30 L\,s$^{-1}$ and 1.05 L\,s$^{-1}$ per metre, $Q_s/q=28.6$ metres of width can be watered at once, so the 120 m field needs 4.2 sets of 401 min, 28.1 h per irrigation. The gross water per irrigation is 169 mm for 72 mm needed, which is the $E_a$ of 43 per cent. In this season the DSS finds no irrigation due, so the totals for the season are zero.")
s+=F('sid_film','0.96',r"Soil water in the days ahead (DSS): the profile of water content, the water held in the root zone through the 31 days and the advice for the day.",r"The film at the bottom of the page is the daily model on which the design rests (Chapter~8). The left panel is the water content of the profile on the chosen day against the wilting point, the trigger and field capacity; the right panel follows the water held in the root zone, coloured by phase (comfortable, nearing the trigger, crop stress), with the rain and irrigation days at the top. The text gives the verdict and the numbers of the season: irrigations, rain, crop water use and drainage. The required depth $Z_n$ used above is the depletion between the trigger and field capacity read from this film.")
open(OUT+'app11.tex','w').write(s)
# ---------------- chapter 12
s=HEAD+r"""The drip layer is organised in the same way as the surface layer: inputs, the wet soil around the emitter, the uniformity of the lateral, the growth of the bulb, the result for the design, the layout of the whole system with its pump and pipes, a table of improvements, a sensitivity chart, the engineering checks and the water of the season. The case shown is an inline-emitter system of 2.0 L\,h$^{-1}$ emitters every 0.50 m on laterals 1.50 m apart and 100 m long, on one hectare of the coarse loam S01 with wheat."""+"\n\n"
s+=F('drip_inputs','0.96',r"Inputs of the drip layer: crop, mulch, emitter type, placement of the lateral, run time and field size, and the hydraulic and farm variables.",r"The buttons choose the emitter family (online, inline, tape, pressure-compensating types) and the placement of the lateral (on the surface or buried at 15, 25, 45 or 55 cm). The run time can be automatic, so that the volume applied equals the requirement divided by the efficiency, or manual. The boxes are the variables of the hydraulic calculation of Section~12.5: emitter spacing $S_e$ and flow $q$, lateral spacing $S_l$, length and inside diameter, inlet pressure head, the irrigation interval, the area, the mainline, the farm supply and the water lift.")
s+=F('drip_moisture','0.96',r"Soil moisture around the emitter: the driest, the average and the wettest emitter, with the soil water profile of the day and where the water goes.",r"Three pictures show the wet soil under the driest 10 per cent of emitters, the average emitter and the wettest 10 per cent, because the discharge of the emitters is not exactly equal (Section~12.4). Blue is water in the root zone, indigo is water that has passed below the roots and yellow is water evaporated at the surface. The graph and bar at the bottom split the water of one irrigation into stored (94 per cent), deep percolation (6 per cent) and evaporation (0 per cent), and the profile of the day shows the trigger and field capacity for reference.")
s+=F('drip_uniform','0.96',r"Uniformity along the lateral: pressure falls with friction, so far emitters deliver less (emission uniformity $E_u=94$ per cent).",r"The curve is the discharge of the emitters along the lateral as a percentage of the nominal discharge (inline emitter, exponent $x=0.5$), together with the fall of pressure head along the pipe from 10.0 m at the inlet to 9.0 m at the end, a loss of 1.0 m. The table gives the manufacturer's coefficient of variation (0.04), the 200 emitters on the lateral and the flow into it (0.107 L\,s$^{-1}$); the emission uniformity $E_u$ is the number that enters the efficiency.")
s+=F('drip_bulb','0.96',r"How the wetted bulb grows with the run time: width and depth of the wet soil under the average emitter against the root zone and the lateral spacing.",r"The curves are the width and depth of the wetted bulb as the irrigation runs (Section~12.6). The dashed lines are the lateral spacing (150 cm) and the root zone (96 cm). After the 1.7 h of the run the bulb is 46 cm wide and 57 cm deep, 31 per cent of the soil volume is wetted and its shape factor (depth over radius) is 1.39; the design question is whether the bulb covers the roots without passing below them.")
s+=F('drip_result','0.96',r"Result for this design: efficiency 93.6 per cent, root zone refilled 100 per cent, uniformity 93.6 per cent, deep percolation 6.4 per cent, evaporation 0.0 per cent, gross depth 4.3 mm for 4 mm.",r"The six cards summarise the design in the same way as the surface layer does: application efficiency $E_a$ (with the 85 per cent target), the fraction of the root zone refilled $E_s$, the uniformity, the losses to deep percolation and surface evaporation, and the gross depth per irrigation.")
s+=F('drip_layout','0.96',r"Layout and infrastructure: source, pump, filter, fertigation, valve and gauge, submain and laterals, and the head along the flow path.",r"The flow chain at the top is the equipment of the system, from the water source (lift 12 m) through the pump (7.1 L\,s$^{-1}$, 3.6 kW) and the 120-mesh filter to the fertigation injector and the zone valve. The plan shows one zone running (66 laterals), drawn to scale on the 100 m wide field; the lower chart is the head at each stage of the path, from the pump at 33.3 m to the lateral end at 9.0 m, and the table gives the totals: 66 laterals and 13\,266 emitters, a pump head of 33.3 m and 6\,699 m of pipe per hectare. The pipe table lists the diameter, length, flow, velocity and head loss of the lateral, the submain and the mainline (Section~12.8).")
s+=F('drip_dtable','0.96',r"Design table: the current design already meets the 85 per cent target ($E_a=93.6$ per cent, $E_s=100$ per cent).",r"The table works like the one of the surface layer: it starts from the current design and, if the target is not met, adds one improvement per row. When the first row already meets the target, as here, the layer says so and leaves the design unchanged.")
s+=F('drip_sens','0.96',r"What the efficiency is most sensitive to: run time ($\pm15.6$ points), emitter spacing and emitter flow.",r"For drip irrigation the run time dominates: a run that is 25 per cent too long loses water below the roots or a run that is too short leaves a deficit. The spacing and the flow of the emitters matter much less, and the lateral spacing, the placement and the depth per irrigation have no effect on $E_a$ in this case because the bulb stays within the roots.")
s+=F('drip_checks','0.96',r"Design checks: pipe, working pressure, filtration, plant water volume and leaching requirement.",r"The checks use the catalogue formulas of the application: the local crop water use $ET_{c,loc}=K_r\,K_c\,ET_0$ with the local reduction factor $K_r$, the depth and volume per plant per interval, the run time, the leaching requirement $LR=EC_w/(2\,EC_e)$ that follows from the salinity of the water and the tolerance of the crop (Rhoades), and the filter and pressure limits of the emitter. Each line says whether the design satisfies the rule.")
s+=F('drip_meaning','0.96',r"What the design means for the season and the farm: peak crop need, system flow per hectare, sets and pumping hours.",r"The table converts the design into the farm quantities: the net irrigation of the season, the gross water per irrigation ($d_g=d_n/E_a$), the peak crop need over the interval, the system flow per hectare (7.41 L\,s$^{-1}$ for 13\,333 emitters and 6\,667 m of lateral), the number of sets for the farm supply and the pumping hours and energy of the season.")
s+=F('drip_film','0.96',r"Soil water in the days ahead (DSS) on which the net depth of the drip design is based.",r"The same daily model as in the surface layer supplies the net depth: the left panel shows the water content of the profile against the trigger, the right panel the water held in the root zone through the following month, coloured by phase. The depth of 4 mm per irrigation of the design is the depletion between the trigger and field capacity read from this film.")
open(OUT+'app12.tex','w').write(s)
# ---------------- chapter 13
s=HEAD+r"""The sprinkler layer is organised in five parts: the site, the sprinkler and its layout, the way the system is run, the farm and its pipes, and the results with the course checks. The case shown is a solid-set system of impact sprinklers with 4.4 mm nozzles at 300 kPa, spaced 12 m along laterals 18 m apart, on 5 ha of coarse loam with a wind of 3 m\,s$^{-1}$; it applies 72 mm per irrigation."""+"\n\n"
s+=F('spr_site','0.96',r"Part A, your site: crop, mulch, soil type with the largest application rate it accepts for the slope, ground slope, wind and surface storage.",r"The first part sets the conditions that limit the sprinkler. The soil tiles give the maximum application rate of each texture at the slope chosen (25 mm\,h$^{-1}$ for coarse loam on a 1.0 per cent slope), which is the rate the sprinkler must not exceed (Section~13.4). The wind speed (3.0 m\,s$^{-1}$) enters the drift loss, and the surface storage (5.0 mm) is the depth of water the soil surface can hold before it runs.")
s+=F('spr_layout','0.96',r"Part B, the sprinkler and its layout: system type, sprinkler type, layout, nozzle, trajectory angle, pressure and spacing, with the live view of the throw.",r"The buttons choose the system (solid-set, hand-move, centre pivot, linear move, travelling gun) and the sprinkler (impact, rotating spray, micro-sprinkler, big gun) and the square or triangular layout. The numerical boxes set the nozzle diameter (4.4 mm), the trajectory angle (25$^\circ$), the pressure (300 kPa) and the spacing along and between laterals. The live view on the left draws the wetted circle (throw radius 15.7 m) and the one on the right the head of the sprinkler with the jet and the droplets; the text under it gives the flow (21.2 L\,min$^{-1}$), the jet speed (14 m\,s$^{-1}$), the biggest drop (3.8 mm) and the loss to wind and air (3.8 per cent).")
s+=F('spr_run','0.96',r"Parts C and D, how you run it and the farm with its water supply and pipes.",r"Part C sets the depth per irrigation (72 mm, from the DSS) and the operating hours per day (16 h); the run time of 805 min is computed. Part D collects the area (5.00 ha), the lateral and mainline lengths, the farm supply (40 L\,s$^{-1}$) and the lift (12 m) from which the pipes and the pump are sized.")
s+=F('spr_plan','0.96',r"Layout in plan: depth infiltrated so far, from the overlapping spray patterns of the sprinklers.",r"The plan shows the sprinklers on the grid and the circles of the spray patterns overlapping. The colours give the depth infiltrated at each point: green is the depth needed, red-brown is short, blue is more than needed and brown hatching is water running off. The overlap of the patterns is the origin of the Christiansen uniformity of Section~13.6.")
s+=F('spr_lateral','0.96',r"Along the lateral: depth applied, depth infiltrated and where it ends up in the soil, with the soil water of the day.",r"The section along the lateral shows the water that arrives at each point (the applied depth, including the drift loss), the depth that infiltrates and the volume of the soil that receives it; below the real texture of the soil the coloured column is the water that infiltrated, the depth divided by the water the soil can hold. The profile of the day at the bottom gives the trigger and field capacity for reference.")
s+=F('spr_intake','0.96',r"Soil intake against the application rate: when the soil cannot keep up, water runs off.",r"The curve is the infiltration rate of the soil with time (the derivative of the Kostiakov intake of Section~13.5) and the horizontal line is the application rate of the sprinklers (5.9 mm\,h$^{-1}$, or 5.7 mm\,h$^{-1}$ after the drift loss). The soil intake at 30 min (19.2 mm\,h$^{-1}$) is well above the application rate, so no runoff occurs; the table lists ponding, runoff, surface storage and pattern uniformity.")
s+=F('spr_result','0.96',r"Result for this design: application efficiency 90.6 per cent, root zone refilled 99.8 per cent, Christiansen uniformity 96.2 per cent, runoff 0, deep percolation 5.6 per cent, drift and evaporation 3.8 per cent.",r"The cards and the bar split the water that leaves the sprinklers into the part stored in the roots (91 per cent), the deep percolation, the drift and evaporation and any runoff; they are the quantities $E_a$, $E_s$ and $CU$ of the chapter.")
s+=F('spr_checks','0.96',r"Does your design pass? The course checks, ten of ten satisfied.",r"The layer applies the rules of the course as ten checks: the spacing along the lateral and between laterals against the wetted radius, the wind limit, the application rate against the maximum for the soil and against the saturated conductivity, the Christiansen uniformity, the lateral head loss (at most 20 per cent of the working head), the mainline gradient, the time to irrigate the whole field within the interval and the flow within the farm supply. A green tick means the rule is met, with the actual value written beside it.")
s+=F('spr_kinds','0.96',r"How a sprinkler works: the kinds of sprinkler system and their efficiency ranges.",r"The teaching part of the layer begins with the kinds of system (solid-set, hand-move, centre pivot, linear move and travelling gun), each with its plan and its range of application efficiency (70 to 75 per cent for solid-set, 65 to 70 per cent for the self-propelled types), and shows where this design falls on the scale.")
s+=F('spr_parts','0.96',r"The sprinkler: parts and pressure class, with the head of a rotary impact sprinkler.",r"The panel names the parts of an impact sprinkler (the swing arm, the spring, the nozzle, the bearing and the riser) and classifies sprinklers by their operating pressure: low pressure (6 to 20 m), medium pressure (20 to 45 m, which includes this design) and high pressure (45 to 100 m, the guns). It repeats the discharge law $q=C_d\,a\sqrt{2gh}$ with $q=k\,h^{0.5}$ and the throw of the jet.")
s+=F('spr_jet','0.96',r"The jet: trajectory angle, throw radius and droplet size.",r"The farthest throw is at about 28 to 30 degrees; low angles (8 degrees) go under foliage and drift less. The panel shows how small droplets fall near the sprinkler and large ones far away (0.5 to 4 mm).")
open(OUT+'app13.tex','w').write(s)
# ---------------- chapter 14
s=HEAD+r"""The drainage layer is a numbered sequence of six panels that answers three questions: how much water must the drains remove, how far apart must they be, and how large must the pipes be. The case shown is a buried pipe drain at 1.20 m in the coarse loam S01, with a barrier 3.0 m below the drains, a field of 300 by 200 m and a pipe slope of 0.15 per cent, designed by the steady-flow (Hooghoudt) method with the discharge taken from the DSS recharge."""+"\n\n"
s+=F('drn_inputs','0.96',r"Inputs of the drainage layer: crop, mulch, drain type (pipe, pipe with control gate, open ditch), design basis (steady flow, falling water table, both), spacing and flow modes, and the numerical variables.",r"The buttons choose the type of drain and the design basis of Section~14.4; spacing and design flow can be automatic (from the equations and the DSS recharge) or manual. The boxes hold the drain depth (1.20 m), the barrier $D$ (3.0 m), the subsoil conductivity as a multiple of that of the topsoil, the time to lower the water table (5.0 d), the field and lateral length, the pipe slope, the critical depth of the water table (0.68 m, from the roots of the crop), the gate height, the ditch width, the salt tolerance of the crop and the lift to the outlet.")
s+=F('drn_answer','0.96',r"The answer in plain words: one pipe drain at 1.20 m every 37 m, 6 pipes of 300 m with 80 mm pipe and a 125 mm collector.",r"The green box states the design as a recommendation a farmer can act on, with the check that the water table stays 0.69 m below the surface, deeper than the 0.68 m the roots need, the number of days too wet in the season (0 of 121), the water carried away by the drains (48 mm), and the reasoning in a sentence.")
s+=F('drn_recharge','0.96',r"1. How much water must the drains remove? Day-by-day deep percolation of the DSS season, from rain and from the part of the irrigation that is lost.",r"The bars are the daily recharge $q$ in mm\,d$^{-1}$ and the dashed line is the design discharge, the value exceeded on only 5 per cent of days (2.0 mm\,d$^{-1}$, the $P_{95}$ of Section~14.3). The table gives the rain of the season (176 mm), the net irrigation, the efficiency of the irrigation design (43 per cent) and the share of the applied water that percolates (7 per cent).")
s+=F('drn_spacing','0.96',r"2. How far apart must the drains be? The spacing $L$ against the design discharge $q$ for three drain depths.",r"The curves are Eq.~(14.x) solved for the spacing: the lower the discharge the wider the spacing, and a deeper drain (green) allows a wider spacing than a shallow one (brown). The red dot is the design ($q=2.0$ mm\,d$^{-1}$, $L=37$ m). The table lists the conductivity above and below the drain (0.32 m\,d$^{-1}$), the drainable pore space (0.193), the effective depth $d=1.86$ m, the rise allowed $h=0.52$ m and the spacings by the two methods (37 m steady, 9 m falling-table).")
s+=F('drn_side','0.96',r"3. Side view between two drains, drawn to scale: free drainage (left) and the pipe with a gate (right).",r"The picture shows the water-filled soil, the critical depth of 0.68 m (red dashed line) and the water table midway between the drains at 1.15 m. Both pictures are the same until a control gate is chosen, when the water table on the right is held higher.")
s+=F('drn_wt','0.96',r"4. Where the water table sits through the season, and the outflow of the drains.",r"The upper chart follows the depth of the water table through the 121 days against the drain level and the critical depth; the lower chart is the outflow of the drains in mm\,d$^{-1}$ against the design value. The table lists the days too wet, the SEW$_{30}$ index, the water drained, the largest outflow, the runoff, the leaching requirement and the water needed for salt.")
s+=F('drn_layout','0.96',r"5. Layout: pipes, collector and outlet, seen from above.",r"The plan shows the six laterals of 300 m with the collector and the ditch; the table lists the flow of a lateral (0.26 L\,s$^{-1}$), the lateral pipe (80 mm), the longest lateral that the pipe can carry (837 m), the collector flow (1.4 L\,s$^{-1}$) and its pipe (125 mm), the trench volume (1\,080 m$^3$) and the pipe laid (2\,000 m). The pipes are sized with Manning's equation ($n=0.016$, corrugated pipe) running at most 80 per cent full.")
s+=F('drn_soils','0.96',r"6. The same drains in every soil: spacing at the design discharge and depth for the ten soils, from their conductivity (Saxton and Rawls).",r"The table repeats the calculation for the ten soils of the application at the same discharge and depth, so the effect of the soil is isolated: the sand needs a spacing of 138 m and the silty clay loam 17 m, a difference of eight times. The row of the chosen soil is highlighted.")
s+=F('drn_result','0.96',r"Result for this design: drain spacing 37 m (333 m per hectare), drainage coefficient 2.0 mm\,d$^{-1}$, water table midway 0.69 m deep, 0 days too wet, 48 mm drained, lateral 80 mm, collector 125 mm.",r"The cards give the result in the units a contractor needs: the length of pipe per hectare, the drainage coefficient in mm\,d$^{-1}$ and in m$^3$ per hectare per day, the depth of the water table midway and whether it meets the critical depth, the days too wet, the volume drained and the diameters of the pipes.")
s+=F('drn_meaning','0.96',r"What this means for the farm: the soil, crop and irrigation in use, the water table to be kept, the water per hectare, the salt balance and the total.",r"The last table puts the answer in farm terms: the field of 6.0 ha needs 6 laterals every 37 m, the drained water is 475 m$^3$ per hectare for the season, and the salt balance says the water of 0.6 dS\,m$^{-1}$ needs a 4 per cent leaching requirement, which the drained water already provides. A note warns when the flow speed in the pipe falls below 0.3 m\,s$^{-1}$, when silt would settle and a steeper slope is needed.")
open(OUT+'app14.tex','w').write(s)
print('ok')
